Z||000005||Spektrum-87GT||10-27-2001||01:44 AM||||although it sounds negative, i agree with blackstang, also look at the malibu. this has been covered before. if the fiero were to still be made, it'd probably be a fwd, 4 banger, maybe have a 3800 option. basically... as its been said before.. a saturn!||||reg||1
Z||000006||AusFiero||10-27-2001||05:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackStang95:<BR><B>Yeah, no telling what gm might have done to it.  Look at the joke the monte carlo and other models have become.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Yep so true. Mustangs were cool until 1970. Look at the joke they became.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero2.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000007||Brian87egotrip||10-27-2001||10:48 AM||||I have to agree. Very well of could have been ugly.  But you never know.<P>------------------<BR>Brian                    87GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||LZeitgeist||10-27-2001||11:02 AM||||Yup, like the 1st Gen MR2 was kinda cool and 'wedge-y', like the Triumph TR-7... the 2nd Gen MR2 was like the car had gone through puberty, filled out and got sexy. 8) <P>The 3rd Gen MR2 is a butt-ugly bastard child. Like a K-Mart edition of a Porsche Boxter designed and built by a five-year old child.<P><BR>If the Fiero had survived this long, there's no telling what it would look like now. The '89 GT prototype looked *awesome*. However, I don't think it *would* have lasted this long, due to <BR>A) pressure from the Corvette lobby, <BR>B) needing its own assembly plant due to the different nature of the design, and <BR>C) direct competition with the Firebird, which would probably be less expensive to continue building over the long term, due to being able to share assembly plants with the Camaro, due to similar design.<P>(I'm going on gut feelings... not sure about the total and complete accuracy of the 'assembly plant' comments up there, but at least I'm pretty sure that's what the case is/would be...)<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||10-27-2001||12:07 PM||||GM probably would have found a way to ruin them in our eyes.  Maybe not, the Corvette people were able to keep that car from going bad, but it certainly wasn't easy.  The C5 almost wasn't even produced.  The Corvette guys in GM had to skim dollars off of the Camaro design budget to get the money to design the C5 behind GM corporates back!  The resulting redesign of the 98 Camaro wasn't much more than new headlights.  Maybe the F-body was sacraficed to save the 'Vette?  <P>One of the things I like the most about the Fiero is its uniqueness, and that it doesn't cost a fortune to keep on the road.  The parts are reasonably priced (except my 88 suspension stuff) and they do turn a lot of heads!||||reg||1
Z||000010||BlackStang95||10-27-2001||12:07 PM||||Yep so true. Mustangs were cool until 1970. Look at the joke they became. <P>Yeah...from 74-82...||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||10-27-2001||12:48 PM||||they made 4cyl mustangs up until the late 80s, not sure how much longer after that.. those weren't exactly ford's finest cars either.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
Z||000012||tomcat06085||10-27-2001||01:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fformula88:<BR><B>GM probably would have found a way to ruin them in our eyes.  Maybe not, the Corvette people were able to keep that car from going bad, but it certainly wasn't easy.  The C5 almost wasn't even produced.  The Corvette guys in GM had to skim dollars off of the Camaro design budget to get the money to design the C5 behind GM corporates back!  The resulting redesign of the 98 Camaro wasn't much more than new headlights.  Maybe the F-body was sacraficed to save the 'Vette?  <P>One of the things I like the most about the Fiero is its uniqueness, and that it doesn't cost a fortune to keep on the road.  The parts are reasonably priced (except my 88 suspension stuff) and they do turn a lot of heads!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The vettes got ugly in the 70's and 80's and the improvements they made in performance were negligible. the biggest improvement was the last two or three years. the vipers came out and couldn't last in the enduro racing, but chrysler fixed them, improved the reliability factor in every respect and then dominated gt and endurance road racing in the ninties. the gt350's dominated the sixties and the cobras were beautiful, but a waste of horsepower if you were a roadracer. the 289 was the best engine ford made in the 60's and seventies...when dodge got the viper program going and dominated roadracing at sebring, daytona, le mans...they all had their days of glory...i loved them all, but i have to give it to the camaro guys, because from the transam days of the sixties to the the gts, gtu, gto days of the present...they are the american car to beat...but don't mess with the bmw's...never owned one, but they are a good car for every occassion<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://jrreeves.com/images/blckfire.jpg"> <BR>tomcat...Thanks for the help!||||reg||12
Z||000013||Formula88||10-27-2001||01:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackStang95:<BR><B>Yep so true. Mustangs were cool until 1970. Look at the joke they became. <P>Yeah...from 74-82...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mustang: II - Fun: 0   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Actually, even the 79 Muscrat had a V8 option.  A crappy 255, but at least it was a V8.  It was kinda cool that they were built off the Fox platform (think Fairmont).  A friend of mine in High School had an 82 or 83 Ford Fairmont Futura (the sporty 2 door :rollseyes <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  We put the 5.0 engine, tranny, rear end, and suspension out of several junked Mustangs into it.  Boy did we surprise a few people with that!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  In the early 80's, there wasn't much that could hang with the 5.0's, so unless it was old muscle - we had a pretty good shot.  Even took a few loaded 5.0 GTs due to their weight disadvantage.10||||reg||6
Z||000014||Monkeyman||10-27-2001||03:43 PM||||I don't agree with any of that, except parts 1,2 & 3.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I loved the early '80s Vettes.  (OK.  So the power was lacking, but they looked great!)  I once owned an '82.  It didn't handle bad and had decent power (those were the luxury coupe years) for the times.  I wish I still had it.  It would make a great daily driver so I could keep the Fiero for nice, sunny days.||||reg||1
